<div class="landing">
  <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
    <span class="eyebrow">THE MATERIAL COMPONENT LIBRARY</span>
    <h1 class="hero__name" id="hero-title">Material Design.<br><span class="hero__accent">Made for the web.</span></h1>
    <p class="hero__tagline">Expressive components. Your framework.<br>A little TypeScript, a lot of possibility.</p>
    <%/* material's buttons, as links: the same classes and parts as an m-button's own <button>
     (material/styles/button.css), so they stay links that open in a new tab and work
     without scripts. */%>
    <div class="hero__actions"><a class="mtrl-button mtrl-button--filled mtrl-button--m mtrl-button--icon mtrl-button--round" href="/components/"><span class="mtrl-button__text">Explore components</span><span class="mtrl-icon mtrl-button__icon mtrl-button__icon--end" aria-hidden="true"><%~ it.icon('arrow_forward') %></span></a><a class="mtrl-button mtrl-button--outlined mtrl-button--m mtrl-button--round" href="/docs/"><span class="mtrl-button__text">Read the docs</span></a><a class="mtrl-button mtrl-button--outlined mtrl-button--m mtrl-button--icon mtrl-button--round" href="https://github.com/floor/material" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span class="mtrl-button__text">Source on GitHub</span><span class="mtrl-icon mtrl-button__icon mtrl-button__icon--end" aria-hidden="true"><%~ it.icon('open_in_new') %></span></a></div>
    <div class="hero__install"><%~ it.install %></div>
    <div class="hero__notes"><span>Material Design 3</span><span>Tree-shakable</span><span>Zero runtime dependencies</span><span>Open source</span></div>
  </section>
  <%/* Why material: three reasons, right after the hero (the owner, 2026-11-25). */%>
  <div class="landing-features landing-features--reasons">
    <div><span class="feature-index">01 / EXPRESSIVE</span><h2>Material 3 Expressive, today.</h2><p>Google's latest Material Design, built for the web: the new shapes, motion and components, in <%= it.shaking.count %> components you can try live.</p></div>
    <div><span class="feature-index">02 / COMPATIBLE</span><h2>One library, every stack.</h2><p>Plain JavaScript factories, web components, and React, Vue, Svelte and SolidJS components, with server rendering. In TypeScript.</p></div>
    <div><span class="feature-index">03 / LIGHTWEIGHT</span><h2>Small by design.</h2><p>Tree-shakable, with zero runtime dependencies: a <%= it.shaking.core %> KB core, then each component adds from <%= it.shaking.min %> KB gzip. <a class="text-link" href="/docs/bundle-size/">See every size</a></p></div>
  </div>
  <%/* Expressive: what M3 Expressive brings, as material ships it. Google's description from
     https://m3.material.io/blog/building-with-m3-expressive (read 2026-11-25); the components'
     notes from the Why material guide's coverage table. */%>
  <section class="shaking shaking--carousel" aria-labelledby="expressive-title">
    <div class="shaking__text">
      <span class="feature-index">EXPRESSIVE</span>
      <h2 id="expressive-title">Material 3 Expressive, built for the web.</h2>
      <p>M3 Expressive is <a class="text-link" href="https://m3.material.io/blog/building-with-m3-expressive">Google's evolution of Material 3</a>: new and updated components, spring motion, and new shapes, colour and type. material brings it to the web: Expressive components such as the button group, split button, FAB menu, loading indicator and toolbar, and Expressive sizes and shapes for buttons and icon buttons.</p>
      <p class="shaking__note"><a class="text-link" href="/docs/why-material/#material-3-and-expressive-coverage">What material ships of M3 Expressive</a></p>
    </div>
    <%/* The Expressive components, live: the catalog's own cards, in its grid. */%>
    <div class="component-grid expressive-grid"><% it.expressiveCards.forEach(card => { %><%~ card %><% }) %></div>
  </section>
  <%/* Compatible: the same component in every way of building. */%>
  <section class="shaking" aria-labelledby="compatible-title">
    <div class="shaking__text">
      <span class="feature-index">COMPATIBLE</span>
      <h2 id="compatible-title">One library, every stack.</h2>
      <p>The same components, the way you build: plain JavaScript factories, web components for any page, and components for React, Vue, Svelte and SolidJS. They render on the server too, and every API is typed.</p>
      <pre class="shaking__code"><code>// React
import { Button } from 'material/react';

&lt;Button variant="filled"&gt;Save&lt;/Button&gt;</code></pre>
      <p class="shaking__note"><a class="text-link" href="/docs/frameworks/">Frameworks guide</a> · <a class="text-link" href="/docs/server-rendering/">Server rendering</a></p>
    </div>
    <ul class="shaking__bars shaking__list" aria-label="Frameworks">
      <% [['Vanilla JS', "import { createButton } from 'material'"], ['Web Components', "import { defineAll } from 'material/elements'"], ['React', "import { Button } from 'material/react'"], ['Vue', "import { MButton } from 'material/vue'"], ['Svelte', "import { Button } from 'material/svelte'"], ['SolidJS', "import { Button } from 'material/solid'"]].forEach(([name, line]) => { %><li><span class="shaking__name"><%= name %></span><code class="shaking__kb"><%= line %></code></li><% }) %>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <%/* Tree-shaking: the measured cost of one component (data/sizes.json, scripts/sizes.ts). */%>
  <section class="shaking" aria-labelledby="shaking-title">
    <div class="shaking__text">
      <span class="feature-index">LIGHTWEIGHT</span>
      <h2 id="shaking-title">Ship only what you use.</h2>
      <p>Every component is its own module, with its own stylesheet. Import a button and your bundler keeps the button: the other components never reach your users.</p>
      <pre class="shaking__code"><code>import { createButton } from 'material';
import 'material/styles/base';
import 'material/styles/button';</code></pre>
      <p class="shaking__note">material's core, <strong><%= it.shaking.core %> KB</strong>, loads once; each component then adds <strong><%= it.shaking.min %>–<%= it.shaking.max %> KB</strong> of JavaScript, gzip, across all <%= it.shaking.count %> components. material's own measurement, on material <%= it.shaking.version %>. <a class="text-link" href="/docs/bundle-size/">Every component's size</a></p>
    </div>
    <table class="shaking__table">
      <caption class="sr-only">What each component adds over material's core, JavaScript, gzip</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Category</th><th scope="col">Components</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody><% it.shaking.groups.forEach(group => { %><tr><th scope="row"><%= group.label %></th><td><%= group.rows.map(row => `${row.name} +${row.kb} KB`).join(', ') %></td></tr><% }) %></tbody>
    </table>
  </section>
  <div class="landing-features">
    <div><span class="feature-index">01 / EXPLORE</span><h2>Find the right expression.</h2><p>Try variants, sizes, and shapes in a live component playground.</p></div>
    <div><span class="feature-index">02 / CUSTOMIZE</span><h2>Make it feel like you.</h2><p>Switch Material themes and light or dark mode. See every change as you make it.</p></div>
    <div><span class="feature-index">03 / BUILD</span><h2>Take it from here.</h2><p>Copy your configuration and bring the component to your project. No framework required.</p></div>
  </div>
  <%~ include('footer', it) %>
</div>
